{{also|Sitzen}}
==German==

===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro|ine-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|sitzen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|sizzen}}, {{m|goh|sitten}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*sittjan}}. Cognate with {{cog|nds|sitten}}, {{cog|nl|zitten}}, {{cog|en|sit}}, {{cog|da|sidde}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈzɪtsn̩/|/ˈzɪtsən/}} {{a|prescriptive standard}}
* {{IPA|de|/ˈsitsn̩/}} {{a|Austria|Bavaria}}
* {{audio|de|De-sitzen.ogg|audio}}
* {{audio|de|De-sitzen2.ogg|audio}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|sitzen<saß,gesessen,säße.haben,sein>}}

# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[sit]]; to [[perch]]
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[stay]] (in one place); to [[remain]]; to [[be]] (in a particular place or state)
#: {{uxi|de|Wir '''saßen''' fest!|We were stuck!}}
# {{senseid|de|clothing: fit}} {{lb|de|intransitive|of clothing}} to [[fit]]
# {{lb|de|intransitive|in certain constructions}} to [[be]] {{i|e.g. with {{m|de|voller}}}}
#: {{uxi|de|Der Schrank '''sitzt''' voller Motten.|The cupboard '''is''' full of moths.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive|colloquial}} to [[do time]]; to [[spend]] time in jail
#: {{uxi|de|im [[Gefängnis]] [[sitzen]].|to be in jail.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive|colloquial|of a strike, a comment, etc.}} to [[hit home]]; to have a significant effect
# {{lb|de|intransitive|Swiss}} to [[sit down]]

====Usage notes====
The most frequent auxiliary with ''sitzen'' is {{m|de|haben}}: ''Ich habe gesessen.'' In northern and central Germany, only this form is used. In southern Germany, Austria, and Switzerland, {{m|de|sein}} is common in the vernacular and also, alternatively, in standard usage: ''Ich bin gesessen.'' However, for some meanings like 5 and 6, ''haben'' is used everywhere. This can lead to misunderstandings.

